  "core_replay_sha256": "f14c66a450d1ce5165ada9c78783f5ea529c6e4e0582b43a0ec5158793ad275b",
  "actual_revised_proof": "For $R=\\diag(r)$, $RMR$ is a symmetric Z-matrix whose $i$th row sum is $r_i(Mr)_i$. For $H=RMR$, the leftmost real point of row $i$'s Gershgorin disc is $H_{ii}-\\sum_{j\\ne i}|H_{ij}|=r_i(Mr)_i$: its off-diagonal entries are nonpositive. Every eigenvalue lies in the union of these discs \\cite{Gershgorin1931}. The quadratic form of $H$ is the sum of nonnegative edge terms $-(RMR)_{ij}(x_i-x_j)^2$ and row-sum terms $r_i(Mr)_ix_i^2$. Congruence gives the claim. To see why G1 and G2 need not be nested, take\r\n\\[\r\nr=(1,2)^\\top,\\qquad M_a=\\begin{bmatrix}1&-1\\\\-1&1\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\r\nM_b=\\begin{bmatrix}2&-1\\\\-1&1/2\\end{bmatrix}.\r\n\\]\r\nHere $M_a\\one=(0,0)^\\top$ and $M_a r=(-1,1)^\\top$, whereas $M_b\\one=(1,-1/2)^\\top$ and $M_b r=(0,0)^\\top$. The first matrix passes only G1, the second only G2. Neither ordering of the two exact optima follows in general.",
  "printed_claims": {
    "identity": true,
    "scale": true,
    "domain": true,
    "union": true
  },
  "symbolic_residual": "Matrix([[0], [0], [0]])",
  "signed_edge_variables": "arbitrary nonnegative weights; positive scaling; arbitrary real diagonals",
  "general_source_proof": "Normalize an eigenvector by an entry of maximal nonzero magnitude. Its row of Hv=lambda*v gives |lambda-Hii| <= sum(j!=i) |Hij|. This chooses at least one disc. H is real symmetric, so its eigenvalues lie on the real line. Hij<=0 implies the left endpoint equals the row sum, ri*(Mr)i. If Mr>=0 every left endpoint is nonnegative; positive congruence transfers H PSD to M PSD.",
